<DetailLayout
  title="Huangpu River Night Cruise — The Best Way to See Shanghai's Two Skylines"
  subtitle="One hour on the water, both banks lit to the skyline. Here is how to pick the right cruise, when to sail for the full effect, and how to get the best seats."
  description="Complete Huangpu River cruise guide: night cruise times, prices, which boat to book, the best sailing time, how to get to the pier by Metro, and how to combine the cruise with the Bund."
  image="/images/shanghai/bund-night.webp"
  category="Tour & Experience"
  {facts}
  {faqs}
  {related}
>
  <p>
    Shanghai is a city of two skylines, and almost nothing shows that better than the <strong>Huangpu River cruise</strong>. From the water, the river becomes a dividing line through the heart of the city: on the west bank, the colonial-era buildings of the <a href="/the-bund">Bund</a> — the customs house, the old banks, the grand hotels — glowing gold against the night; on the east bank, the Pudong megatowers — the Oriental Pearl, the Jin Mao, and the twisting <a href="/shanghai-tower">Shanghai Tower</a> — rising in electric light. From a boat between them, you finally understand why everyone photographs this city at night.
  </p>
  <p>
    The cruise is simple, dependable, and easy to fit into an evening. This guide covers the practical decisions — which sailing, when, how to book, how to get there — so the hour on the water is pure payoff.
  </p>

  <h2>Which cruise to choose</h2>
  <p>
    The standard offering is a <strong>1-hour cruise</strong> that leaves from the main piers on the south Bund (primarily <strong>Shiliupu Pier</strong>, near the old customs house) and loops between the Bund and Pudong, passing under the Nanpu and Yangpu bridges. This is the one to take: it covers the full skyline panorama, and the open upper decks give you unobstructed views and photo lines.
  </p>
  <p>
    Upgrades to consider:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>VIP top-deck seating</strong> — a reserved seat on the open upper deck instead of standing; worth the small premium if you want to sit with a view.</li>
    <li><strong>Dinner cruises</strong> — skip these unless you specifically want dinner afloat; you pay a significant markup for food that is usually unremarkable, and the scenery is the same.</li>
    <li><strong>Short morning/day cruises</strong> — calmer and cheaper, with good daylight views, but they miss the night show. If you can only do one, do the night one.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>When to sail</h2>
  <p>
    The definitive experience is a sailing <strong>after full darkness</strong>. The windows change with the seasons:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Summer (June–August):</strong> lights come on around 7:00 PM; aim for a 7:00–8:30 PM sailing.</li>
    <li><strong>Winter (December–February):</strong> lights come on around 5:30 PM; a 6:00–7:30 PM sailing is ideal.</li>
    <li><strong>Spring and autumn:</strong> 6:30–8:00 PM, catching the dusk transition if you want it.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    A sailing about 30 minutes after sunset gives you the best of both: the last glow of dusk over the river, then the city switching on. Weekday evenings are quieter; weekend and holiday sailings are busier and sell out sooner.
  </p>

  <h2>Getting to the pier</h2>
  <p>
    The main boarding point is <strong>Shiliupu Pier</strong> on the south Bund. The easiest Metro access is <strong>Yuyuan Garden Station (Line 10), Exit 1</strong> — a 10-minute walk through the historic docks district, with the pier clearly signed. From the Bund promenade itself, walk south along the riverfront about 15 minutes; from East Nanjing Road (Line 2), take the tunnel crossing or a short taxi. The pier has ticket offices and snack vendors; arrive <strong>30–45 minutes before your sailing</strong> to board calmly and grab a good spot.
  </p>

  <h2>What you will see</h2>
  <p>
    Leaving the pier, the boat swings north along the Bund side first, giving you a low-angle, up-close pass of the colonial facades — the customs house clock tower, the HSBC Building, the Peace Hotel — all floodlit gold. Then the boat crosses to the Pudong side, and the perspective flips: now you are looking at the megatowers head-on, the Oriental Pearl's pink spheres and the Shanghai Tower's glass spiral filling the frame. The commentary (recorded, in English among other languages) points out the landmarks as you pass. Mid-route you slip under the illuminated bridges, which are themselves part of the show.
  </p>
  <p>
    Practical notes for the deck: it is noticeably cooler and windier over the water than on the street, especially in the evening — bring a jacket even in summer. The best photo spot is the bow on the upper deck; the stern is more sheltered if the wind bites. Hold onto hats and phone straps; the breeze is persistent.
  </p>

  <h2>Combining the cruise with your evening</h2>
  <p>
    The classic evening combines the cruise with the Bund promenade. A good plan: arrive at the Bund around 6:00 PM, walk the riverside as the light fades, take a 7:00–7:30 PM sailing from Shiliupu, and finish with dinner in the Old City or back on the Bund's restaurant stretch. It is one of the most satisfying self-contained evenings in Shanghai, and it layers perfectly onto a day that includes <a href="/yu-garden">Yu Garden</a> and the historic district.
  </p>

  <h2>Practical notes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Price:</strong> standard tickets run RMB 100–150 per person; VIP deck seating adds roughly RMB 50–100.</li>
    <li><strong>Booking:</strong> book online a day ahead on weekends and holidays; walk-up is usually fine on weekday evenings.</li>
    <li><strong>Cash and mobile pay:</strong> Alipay, WeChat Pay, and bank cards are all accepted at the ticket office.</li>
    <li><strong>Duration:</strong> about 60 minutes afloat, plus boarding time. The full loop is worth taking once.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The Huangpu River cruise is the rare tourist classic that fully delivers. It turns Shanghai's two skylines — the colonial past and the megacity future — into one unbroken panorama, and it does it from the only place you can see them together: the water between them. Book the night sailing, dress for the deck, and enjoy the hour.
  </p>
